      "body": "# Description\n\nWith `weight_deosc_state_slabs`, every DEOSC shard gets four views (`prev`, `prev_q`, `dist_w`, `dist_w_qdq`) into two per-device slabs. This change pads each shard so its views start at the same phase modulo 32 as the shard's offset in its parameter (`rng.start % 32`). The fused DEOSC kernels then see whole 32-column MXFP4 tiles at aligned addresses, including shards that start mid-tile. The padding is allocated once, and state shapes and checkpoint contents are unchanged.\n\nThis is Su Ann Chong's commit from `suachong/gptoss-deosc-state-alignment-20261011` (8488dc54c, also on `suachong/mpo`). It applies to `main` unchanged. The other commits on this branch add:\n\n- a CPU test for shards that start at offset 0 and mid-tile (offset 45): views stay inside their slab and keep the logical tile phase;\n- black formatting of the change.\n\nIn the GPT-OSS-20B MLPerf 6.1 tracking deck, this change together with the router-gradient join (#1372) takes the MXFP4 recipe from 56.49 to 56.11 min TTT (67.88 → 68.33 samples/s) on 8× MI355X.\n\n## Validation\n\nValidation is in progress on 8× MI355X with the `main` CI image stack (v26.8 base, Primus-Turbo d04ffd9b):\n\n- [ ] `tests/unit_tests/backends/megatron/test_weight_deosc.py` and `test_weight_deosc_gpu.py` on the GPU\n- [ ] GPT-OSS-20B MXFP4 MLPerf 200-step short test (train and eval loss decrease, step-30 trace)\n- [ ] full convergence to eval loss 3.34\n\n",
